%T A398713 124,68,130,592,884,128,6,2,27,15,33714176,4,315,16,256,6,2,38,944,14,
%U A398713 656,1012,8,18632,512,6,2,33,22,232,52,4,110,1155,32,1024,6
%N A398713 Table read by descending antidiagonals: T(n, k) = smallest number m such that 2*(n*m+k) = Sum_{d|m} (n*d+k), n>=0, k>=0.
%C A398713 T(n, k) is the smallest m such that 2*n*m = sigma(m) * n + k*(tau(m)-2).
%C A398713 If T(8, 3) or T(5, 6) exist they are bigger than 7883969218801815621081600.
%C A398713 Each natural number is L-perfect for exactly one linear equation L(m) = n*m+k, where n and k are coprime integers and both are not 0.
%C A398713 T(n, k) is the smallest L-perfect number, where L(m) = n*m+k.
%C A398713 A000396 are n*m-perfect for n > 0 (that's why left column is 6), A000040 are n perfect for n>0 (that's why top row is 2), A066229 are n*(m+1)-perfect numbers for n>0 (the diagonal is always 4).
%C A398713 2^n is always an T(n-1, 1)-perfect number (including n = 0), and it appears that it is also the smallest such number.
%C A398713 It is unknown if T(n, k) always exists or if there are infinitely many n*m+k-perfect numbers. As far as I know only for k-perfect numbers (primes) it is proven that there are infinitely many of them. For some, it seems that only 1 such number exists (2*n+1-perfect for example), but it's possible that next such number is very large.
%H A398713 Max A. Alekseyev, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17832">Computing bounded solutions to linear Diophantine equations with the sum of divisors</a>, arXiv:2601.17832 [math.NT], 2026. See section 5.3.
%F A398713 T(n, k) = T(n/gcd(n, k), k/gcd(n, k)).
